WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.740
Whether you're a fan of the card game itself
or you're in it for the story of restoring

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.540
an ancient pharaoh's long-lost memories, there
are plenty of little details from Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.760
that casual fans could have easily missed.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.460
Like many popular anime, Yu-Gi-Oh! started
life as a manga, serialized in the popular

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.940
manga magazine Weekly Shonen Jump.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.800
In this original manga, creator Kazuki Takahashi
had a somewhat different vision for the series

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.040
than what appears on screen.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.670
Protagonist Yugi Muto, whose name was changed
to Yugi Moto in the English dub, is a bullied

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.720
high school student.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.950
His grandfather, who owns a game shop, allows
him to attempt to put together an ancient

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.240
relic known as the Millennium Puzzle which
Yugi, of course, solves.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.720
This should all sound familiar to fans so
far.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.420
Where the manga series takes a turn is that
the alter ego that possesses Yugi after the

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.820
Puzzle's completion isn't an unwaveringly
just game master.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.000
Instead, this dark entity doles out vigilante
justice against bullies and criminals, going

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.280
so far as to permanently disfigure, brainwash,
or dispatch them.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:12.700
These early chapters of the manga don't focus
just on the card game "Magic &amp; Wizards," as

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.980
it was originally known, but instead rotate
between various diverse games of chance and

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:18.500
skill.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.560
When an opponent challenges this dark Yugi,
it's a bit like challenging Death to a game

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.040
of chess, with impossible stakes and unsavory
outcomes.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.240
"Sorry death, you lose!

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:33.860
It was professor Plum!"

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.000
This early Yu-Gi-Oh! is much more of a horror
series than what it would become later in

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:40.009
its serialization.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:45.590
The anime series that is most popular in the
Western world is not the only Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.080
anime.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.780
Before NAS and Studio Gallop had their go
at making one of the most popular shonen anime

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:57.360
of the 2000s, Toei Animation produced what
has come to be known by some fans as "Season

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:57.860
Zero."

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.440
This late-'90s attempt has never had an official
English-language release, unfortunately, but

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.280
even Western fans are aware of how it differs
from what the majority of us know as Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.560
Season Zero hews more closely to the manga's
original story, exploring those dark themes

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.080
of vigilante justice meted out by a cruel
and savvy games master.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.280
It also sticks more closely to Takahashi's
original art style, which in the early days

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.800
was distinguished by his characters' cuter,
more rounded features.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.540
Since licensors have gone this long without
attempting to bring this anime stateside,

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.579
we don't think it's likely to ever find its
way into the North American market.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.380
Still, it's a nifty piece of the history of
the series and its many iterations.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:46.680
In the early manga and the original 1998 anime adaptation, there is a brief story about a

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.160
character with a penchant for American superheroes.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:56.460
Hanasaki, a classmate of Yugi's is obsessed
with the American superhero comic "Zombire."

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.640
Zombire definitely seems to be a bit of a
spoof on the character Spawn, and it is clear

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.650
from Takahashi's story that he has a good
understanding of what the American comics

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.700
landscape of the mid-'90s was like.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:14.360
In fact, he and comics creator Mike Mignola
even did an art trade, swapping Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.100
and Hellboy mash-up pieces.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.360
More recently, Takahashi has provided the
artwork for Japanese-only Marvel comics involving

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.940
Spider-Man and Iron Man.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.700
It's clear that Takahashi is a fan of Western
comics, and influenced by them as much as

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.840
he is by other manga.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.440
Whenever something is translated for a different
audience, things end up changing.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.120
However, hardcore Yu-Gi-Oh! fans are often
disgruntled about some of the changes that

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.900
occurred between the Japanese version of the
series and the English adaptation by 4Kids

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:43.400
Entertainment.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.860
The former was intended for a young teenage
audience, while 4Kids, naturally, tried to

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.480
market it to younger kids.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.140
To start out, the majority of the characters
had their names changed from their original

00:03:55.150 --> 00:04:03.400
Japanese to more English-friendly names: Jounouchi
was changed to Joey, Honda to Tristan, Anzu

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.960
to Tea, and so on.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.310
This is a common enough practice when anime
is intended for younger audiences, but surely

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:14.749
there are Japanese kids in America who would
love to hear names like theirs on their favorite

00:04:14.749 --> 00:04:16.060
television programs.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:22.340
Beyond this, the English adaptation refers
to the Millennium Puzzle as 5000 years old,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.920
whereas the original Japanese has it at a
more recent 3000 years old.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:32.560
The opening and closing songs in Japanese
are well-written pop songs, but American kids

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.400
were rocking out to a high-powered, largely
wordless tune.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.920
Perhaps the most shocking change of all is
the addition, in English, of something called

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:42.760
the "Shadow Realm."

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.540
When Yami Yugi duels opponents and wins, he
occasionally sends their minds to a place

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.540
referred to by this ominous name.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.460
In the original, however...villains just died.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.860
There was no sugar-coating the outcomes of
these duels in Japanese.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.789
"My millenium puzzle protects me from your
cowardly assault, but nothing will protect

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:06.060
you from OBLIVION!"

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.620
It's easy to get distracted by all the card-playing
going on in Yu-Gi-Oh!, but the truth is that

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.760
there is a ton of fascinating lore hiding
in the background.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.020
Viewers know that the Millennium Puzzle is
an ancient artifact with dark powers, and

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:25.160
at the beginning of the series it seems as
though it brings out a cool, commanding alter-ego

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:25.760
of Yugi.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:31.700
As the series progresses, however, it becomes
clear that this alter ego is his own person,

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.120
albeit one who cannot remember who he is.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.860
Though most of the other Millennium Items
contain great dark power, there is only one

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.900
other, the Millennium Ring, which also houses
a separate entity.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.660
The Ring is worn by Ryo Bakura, a likable
and timid young man with a penchant for the

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.160
macabre.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:56.120
When under the influence of the Ring, he becomes
an entity known as Yami Bakura, an evil character

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.020
who tries to thwart Yugi's efforts at every
turn.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.820
As Yugi and Bakura deal with their own dark
personalities, it becomes more and more obvious

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:08.480
that they are harboring two individuals who
have a long history together.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.740
In the final season, Yami Bakura challenges
Yami Yugi to a game set in ancient Egypt,

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:17.370
where the story of their conflict is played
out.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:21.800
The stakes involve Yami Yugi's lost memory...and
the fate of the world itself.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.800
"Are you saying that everything we do affects
the events of the past?

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.140
ARE YOU?!"

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.260
When the majority of people think of Yu-Gi-Oh!,
they remember the series that is centered

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.980
solely on Yugi and his friends.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:37.960
But even though the initial viewers grew up
and moved on to other franchises, the success

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:42.300
of the series and its real-world trading card
game inspired a slew of spinoffs.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:48.060
Each new iteration keeps the central theme
of dueling with cards, but adds its own spin

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.060
or unique monster types to keep the action
fresh and the audience involved.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.720
The first of these spinoffs, Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:00.259
GX, follows Judai Yuki, called Jaden Yuki
in English, as he makes his way through the

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.920
prestigious Duel Academy.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.140
As a connection to the first series, the school
was founded by the original series' antagonist,

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.600
the hyper-rich super-genius Seto Kaiba.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:17.100
Since viewers seemed to enjoy that, four more
series followed in quick succession: Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.350
5D's, Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.460
Zexal, Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.020
Arc-V, and Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:22.840
VRAINS.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.800
With each new installation comes a further
focus on the intersection of dueling and tech,

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:31.919
a theme which has been part of the series
ever since Seto Kaiba stepped onto the scene

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.120
with his Duel Disks.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.819
There is also a new, as of yet unnamed, spinoff
series set to premiere in 2020, four years

00:07:38.819 --> 00:07:43.820
after VRAINS hit TV, and it's anybody's guess
as to how the gameplay will evolve from its

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:45.700
most recent digital-world makeover.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:48.280
As we now know, Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:52.440
hasn't always been about the card game, despite
what the merchandising team wants you to think.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.680
Even though the most popular anime series
focuses significantly on the cards and their

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:03.400
lore and creation, tabletop roleplaying games
also play a significant role in the plot itself.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.160
In the manga, the shy new kid on the block,
Bakura, invites Yugi and his friends over

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:14.389
to play a game of Monster World, a sort of
in-series version of Dungeons &amp; Dragons.

00:08:14.389 --> 00:08:19.539
During this game, the spirit of the Millennium
Ring takes hold of Bakura, changing the stakes

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:24.669
so that any false move sees one of the players
sealed inside their individual game piece

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:26.380
unless Yugi can break them out.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:31.530
This storyline was pretty violent, which was
probably why it was adapted for the Season

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.240
Zero anime but never made it into the later
adaptations.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.800
Plus, as we mentioned earlier, the final season
of the original series saw Yami Bakura as

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:46.520
game master once again, taking Yami Yugi on
a trip down ancient memory lane in a tabletop

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.360
campaign called Memory World.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:52.860
Most people, especially kids, don't really
think twice about how their anime gets to

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:53.380
them.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.540
The fact is, there's a lot of time, negotiation,
and work that goes into acquiring the rights

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.370
to air an anime in another country, plus all
the work that goes into voice acting and making

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.300
the series presentable to a new audience.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.480
And sometimes, there are issues that arise
as a result of licensing.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:17.100
In 2011, American licensing company 4Kids
Entertainment was sued by TV Tokyo and Nihon

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.960
Ad Systems for breach of contract.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.390
The conflict arose around 4Kids withholding
funds from the licensors that they earned

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:27.800
through deals with companies like Funimation
and Cartoon Network.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:33.170
4Kids ended up filing for Chapter 11 bankruptcy
following this suit and then claimed to have

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.340
bounced back from it in 2012.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.760
However, they filed again in 2016.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.680
Fortunately, 4Kids legal troubles hasn't stopped
Yu-Gi-Oh from being available to stream.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:47.490
The series is available through streaming
on Funimation's website, as well as through

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.440
Crunchyroll, Netflix, and Hulu.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.860
Unsurprisingly, dueling in the real world
doesn't quite match up with dueling as it's

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.220
represented in the anime.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.010
For one thing, while we call them Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:02.660
cards, the characters in-show refer to their
decks as Duel Monsters cards.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:07.880
And in both the manga, and all of the anime
iterations of the series, the rules of Duel

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.100
Monsters are pretty fast and loose.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.720
In order to make a playable game, more structured
rules have been set for those of us playing

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:15.660
along at home.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.040
Another key difference is the cards themselves,
however.

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:24.900
Everyone recognizes the Dark Magician or Blue-Eyes
White Dragon cards, but there are a handful

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:29.120
of cards from the series that either never
made it into the physical realm, or are no

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.020
longer usable when playing by competition
rules.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:37.680
Despite its frequent use, and explanation,
in the series, the spell card Pot of Greed

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.740
is no longer acceptable in a real-life match.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:45.120
Bakura's favorite card, Change of Heart, is
also a no-go, unfortunately.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:48.140
If you want to use these in a real match,
well…

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:52.320
"And this time you don't stand a ghost of
a chance."

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:56.220
Using all your allowance on Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.510
cards as a kid was a common enough experience.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.420
But as we age, it becomes less viable to spend
money that way, never mind finding the time

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.220
and friends with which to duel.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:09.080
Luckily, there are other means of engaging
with the series that can be enjoyed at any

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:10.740
age, and within any budget.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:16.000
Like all successful anime series, Yu-Gi-Oh!
has spawned several video games.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:20.600
Many of these games haven't made it out of
Japan, but there are a few which have.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:22.800
The first was Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.010
Dark Duel Stories for the Game Boy Color,
which pitted the player against characters

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:31.420
from the series in order to progress and acquire
new cards, pretty straightforward.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:32.900
Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:35.820
Duelists of the Roses, however, takes a different
tack.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:40.660
In this game, players chose the side of the
House of Lancaster or the House of York and

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.540
duel in a plot that is loosely based on the
real-world War of the Roses.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.620
The gameplay unfolds on a playing map that
has different terrain, helping or hindering

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.340
certain monsters in those areas.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:56.339
This game expands upon what fans know from
the series to create a new challenge.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:00.760
Of course, games and game systems cost money,
but most people have a phone or tablet these

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:01.260
days.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:06.080
With those devices, you can download the official
Konami app Yu-Gi-Oh!

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:10.600
Duel Links, which allows players to duel characters
or real players from across the world in real

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.640
time, keeping the spirit of one-on-one matches
alive.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.260
In other words, it's always:

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:20.700
"It's time to d-d-d-d-d-duel!"

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.300
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.760
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
anime are coming soon.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:30.440
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

